---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/security/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/security/',
    name: 'sitrep security and privacy',
    description:
      'sitrep is local-first: fully functional with no account and no network, no telemetry by default. Licensing is verified offline and never phones home to stay valid. The sync design is end-to-end encrypted with user-held keys, published before it ships, and verifiable - including bring-your-own storage.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const principles = [
  {
    title: 'Local-first, forever',
    body: 'sitrep is fully functional with no account and no network - not as a trial mode, as the product. Local features are never metered, never expire, and never check entitlements. Sync, when it ships, is an optional layer on top; cancelling it leaves the local app whole.',
  },
  {
    title: 'No telemetry by default',
    body: 'The app ships with no analytics SDKs. Crash reports are opt-in and contain no document content and no paths beyond the app’s own internals. What the app would ever send over the network is short enough to list, and it is listed on this page.',
  },
  {
    title: 'One narrow write path',
    body: 'Exactly three convention-shaped operations - set a status, set a due date, mark a decision superseded - can write to your documents, each behind a preview and a content-hash guard. No other code path writes to your files, ever.',
  },
  {
    title: 'We cannot read what we relay',
    body: 'The planned sync layer is end-to-end encrypted with keys generated on your device and never sent to us; the server stores ciphertext. The protocol is published so the claim can be checked against a document rather than our sincerity.',
  },
  {
    title: 'No key escrow, no reset backdoor',
    body: 'There is no recovery service on our side. Lose every device and the recovery key, and the mirror is gone - your local corpus is never at risk, and the mirror rebuilds from your desktop in minutes. An escrow would turn "we cannot read your data" into marketing copy.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Claims are checkable',
    body: 'Every claim on this page maps to a verification path above: cut the network, read the protocol, bring your own storage. If the implementation ever diverges from this page, that is a bug with the same priority as one in code.',
  },
];

const serverSees = [
  'The email address on the sync account',
  'How many devices are paired',
  'Encrypted blob sizes and timestamps',
];

const serverCannotSee = [
  'File names',
  'File contents',
  'Extracted state - statuses, decisions, due dates',
  'Project names or structure',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security and privacy - sitrep"
  description="How sitrep handles your files: local-first with no account, no network and no telemetry by default, one guarded write path, offline-verifiable licensing that never bricks, and a published end-to-end-encrypted sync design you can verify."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Security &amp; privacy</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Private by architecture, not by policy
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        <Sitrep /> is an AI project status dashboard that reads the markdown on
        your disk - which means it deserves more scrutiny than most apps, not
        less. This page states what it does with your files, what any
        future server could and could not see, and how you can verify each
        claim instead of taking our word for it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="today">
      <h2 id="today" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What we hold today: nothing</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          <Sitrep /> is a local-first desktop app. There is no account, no server
          component, and no telemetry by default. Your files are indexed into
          a local database on your machine and never leave it. The complete
          list of data we hold about you is: nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The only network traffic the shipped app produces is a static
          update-manifest check - and sync, if you one day explicitly enable
          it, under the design at the bottom of this page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="reads">
      <h2 id="reads" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        What it reads, including outside your folders
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Almost everything <Sitrep /> knows comes from the folders you point
          it at: the markdown inside them, and each file's position in git.
          There is one read outside those folders, and it is worth stating
          plainly rather than letting you discover it.
        </p>
        <p>
          To tell "nobody has touched this project in months" apart from "the
          docs are old but the work is live", <Sitrep /> needs to know when a
          project last moved - and git is a poor proxy, because the
          in-progress work is disproportionately untracked or gitignored. So
          it reads two things under <code
            class="rounded bg-fill px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.9em]">~/.claude</code
          >: the timestamps in <code
            class="rounded bg-fill px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.9em]">history.jsonl</code
          >, and the modification times of the session transcript files.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it keeps from that is four columns: a workspace, a project, one
          timestamp, and which of the two sources it came from. The transcripts
          themselves are never opened. The prompt text in the history file is
          parsed and thrown away. None of it is served on any screen, exported,
          or sent anywhere - it feeds the staleness signals and nothing else,
          and per-session scratch directories are deliberately not read at all.
          Setting <code class="rounded bg-fill px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.9em]"
            >sessionEvidence.enabled</code
          > to false turns the whole thing off.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How to verify, instead of trusting</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        "Private" and "encrypted" are claims every product makes. These are
        the paths by which <Sitrep />'s can be checked.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-8 grid max-w-3xl gap-7">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
          <dt class="font-medium">Cut the network</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
            Run <Sitrep /> with Wi-Fi off, or deny it network access outright with
            an outbound firewall like Little Snitch or LuLu. Everything works:
            indexing, extraction, the overview, search, the reader. An app
            that is fully functional offline has no covert channel to lose
            your data through.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
          <dt class="font-medium">Read the traffic</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
            The shipped app's outbound connections are few enough to
            enumerate: a static update-manifest fetch, and sync endpoints if
            you enabled sync. Watch them with any proxy or firewall; anything
            else appearing is a bug we want reported.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
          <dt class="font-medium">Read the protocol</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
            The sync protocol will be public from day one - what is encrypted
            with what, what the server stores, what pairing exchanges. The
            "we cannot read your data" claim should be checkable against a
            document, not against our sincerity.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
          <dt class="font-medium">Bring your own storage</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
            The sync client will speak the same protocol to any S3-compatible
            endpoint, including one you run yourself - and you can inspect the
            ciphertext it writes there. A hosted service is honest when
            leaving it is easy.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
          <dt class="font-medium">Independent audit</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
            A third-party audit of the sync client is planned once the sync
            layer has the revenue to justify one. Until then, the published
            protocol and BYO-storage path exist precisely so you do not have
            to wait for our auditors.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="principles">
      <h2 id="principles" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The principles</h2>
      <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          principles.map(({ title, body }) => (
            <li class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-1.5">
              <h3 class="font-medium">{title}</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">{body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="licensing">
      <h2 id="licensing" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Trial and licensing, offline</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          <Sitrep /> is a paid desktop app, and paid apps usually mean phone-home
          license servers. <Sitrep />'s licensing ships the other way, and it is
          verifiable the same way as the rest: activate a license, then cut
          the network. Nothing degrades.
        </p>
        <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2.5 pl-5 text-sm">
          <li>
            The 14-day trial is the full app, locally: no account, no
            sign-up, no network required to start reading your own files.
          </li>
          <li>
            A license key is a cryptographically signed file (ed25519),
            verified on your machine. The app can check that your license is
            valid with the network cable unplugged - forever.
          </li>
          <li>
            Activation pings are optional and fail open, with a long offline
            grace period. The app never bricks because our server is down,
            your network is down, or we have a bad week.
          </li>
          <li>
            Local features never check entitlements at all, and payment runs
            through a merchant of record: we never see or store your card
            details.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="sync-design">
      <h2 id="sync-design" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The sync design</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Sync - a read-only, encrypted mirror of parsed state and docs on a
          phone or second machine - is <strong class="font-medium text-ink">not built yet</strong>.
          The design is decided and published now, before any backend exists,
          so it can be judged before you are asked to trust it. The short
          version: keys are generated on your device and never sent to us,
          and the server stores ciphertext.
        </p>
      </div>
      <details class="group mt-6 max-w-prose">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-sm font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">
          <span class="group-open:hidden">Read the full sync design - keys, pairing, metadata, share links</span>
          <span class="hidden group-open:inline">The full sync design</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
          <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2.5 pl-5 text-sm">
            <li>
              One symmetric account key, generated on your first device and
              never sent to the server. All content is encrypted on your device
              (XChaCha20-Poly1305 via libsodium) before anything is uploaded;
              the server stores ciphertext.
            </li>
            <li>
              New devices pair over a QR / short code: the existing device
              approves and hands over the wrapped key directly. No passphrase
              ceremony required at onboarding.
            </li>
            <li>
              Optional hardening for the security-conscious: a user passphrase
              (Argon2id-wrapped key) and a printable recovery key - offered
              once, skippable, with a plain warning that we cannot reset it.
            </li>
            <li>
              Metadata is minimized, not just content: blob names are HMAC-keyed
              ids and the sync manifest itself is encrypted, so the server
              cannot infer your project structure from the shapes it stores.
            </li>
            <li>
              A read-only share link encrypts the shared subset with a per-link
              key that travels in the URL fragment - which never reaches the
              server. Revoking the link deletes the blobs.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <div class="mt-4 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
              <h3 class="font-medium text-ink">What the server can see</h3>
              <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
                {
                  serverSees.map((item) => (
                    <li class="flex gap-3">
                      <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-ink-faint select-none">&bull;</span>
                      {item}
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
              <h3 class="font-medium text-ink">What the server cannot see</h3>
              <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
                {
                  serverCannotSee.map((item) => (
                    <li class="flex gap-3">
                      <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-ink-faint select-none">&times;</span>
                      {item}
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm">
            When sync ships, this page will restate that list against the
            actual implementation. If the two ever disagree, the page is wrong
            or the product is - either one is treated as a bug.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="this-site">
      <h2 id="this-site" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">This website</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        sitrep.md sets no cookies and tracks no individuals - the only
        measurement is Cloudflare's cookieless, aggregate Web Analytics (page
        views and referrers) - which is why there is no consent banner: there
        is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
